## 6. Replace MLM pressure with ethical assisted commerce

The incumbents’ advantage is not merely distribution. It is human trust, demonstration, familiarity, and follow-up. A D2C brand needs to recreate those benefits without adopting the harmful aspects of multilevel selling.

### Community Beauty Partner program

Create a single-level referral and consultation program with:

- No joining fee;
- No minimum inventory purchase;
- No requirement to recruit others;
- No commissions on recruited sellers;
- No income promises;
- No status based on personal purchases;
- Direct brand fulfillment;
- Clear fixed or percentage commissions;
- Training in products, shade selection, claims, disclosures, and consumer protection;
- Unique referral links or customer-consented advisor attribution;
- Limits on unsolicited messaging;
- Easy consumer reassignment or opt-out;
- Auditing of health claims and before-and-after content.

Partners could include:

- Makeup artists;
- Beauty students and professionals;
- Micro-creators;
- Salon professionals where appropriate;
- Existing community organizers;
- High-engagement customers.

Partners should be compensated for legitimate education, content, consultation, and attributable sales—not recruitment.

### Consumer promise

Publish a clear statement:

- Advisors never need to hold stock;
- Prices are public;
- Consumers can return products directly to the brand;
- Advice is optional;
- Sponsorship and commissions are disclosed;
- A consumer may shop without an advisor;
- Harassment or misleading claims can be reported.

This becomes a meaningful communications distinction from high-pressure selling models.

