## Key decisions

| Decision | Recommended posture |
|---|---|
| Regional brand model | One core brand, three locally designed commercial systems |
| Asian heritage | Use as a specific formulation proof point, not an exotic or superior beauty ideal |
| Initial assortment | Focused hero products and simple routines |
| Complexion | Launch only after locally representative shade and undertone testing |
| Owned versus marketplace | Use both; owned for loyalty and data, marketplaces for reach and trust |
| Social commerce | Make education, demonstration, and assisted purchase central |
| MLM competition | Recreate human advice through single-level ambassadors without recruitment or inventory loading |
| Mexico | Trend discovery, marketplaces, convenience, creators, accessible premium positioning |
| Colombia | Trust, WhatsApp, consultation, routine value, and community commerce |
| Brazil | Portuguese-native product, creative, operations, and broad representation |
| Influencers | Portfolio of reach, authority, community, and consumer creators |
| Pricing | Local-currency accessible premium with minis and transparent bundles |
| Promotions | Controlled and credible; avoid constant discounting and false scarcity |
| Claims | Local regulatory approval, transparent evidence, no colorism or medical exaggeration |
| Launch sequence | Mexico, then Colombia, then Brazil when independently ready |
| Success criterion | Repeat purchase, trust, product fit, and contribution margin—not launch impressions alone |

## Conclusion

The strongest entry strategy is not a uniform Latin American launch and not a purely digital rejection of incumbent beauty-selling models. Mexico, Colombia, and Brazil each require a distinct combination of product adaptation, marketplaces, owned commerce, creators, payments, and human advice.

The brand should preserve a consistent promise of formulation quality and transparent performance while localizing shades, routines, language, representation, creators, and channel roles. Its key competitive innovation should be **ethical assisted commerce**: the trust and personal guidance consumers value from established beauty networks, without recruitment incentives, inventory pressure, concealed prices, or exaggerated income claims.

If the company proves that products were developed with local consumers rather than merely promoted to them, it can turn Asian heritage into durable credibility rather than short-lived novelty.
